---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const repositoryURL = "https://github.com/drimini-software/release-evidence";
const projectFacts = [
  ["Project", "Release Evidence"],
  ["Status", "Experimental source preview"],
  ["Published", "October 2026"],
  ["Stack", "Go, TypeScript, local web UI"],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Release Evidence experimental project"
  description="Release Evidence is Drimini's experimental local-first workbench for reviewing repository evidence and human release decisions."
  image="/brand/release-evidence-og.png"
  imageAlt="Release Evidence — an experimental local-first project by Drimini."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero shell">
      <div class="case-hero__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Project / Experimental open source</p>
        <h1>Making release evidence reviewable.</h1>
        <p class="case-hero__lede">
          Release Evidence examines one local software repository and helps a
          technical owner assemble the facts and human judgments behind a release
          or handover decision.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="case-disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
        <strong>This is a working experiment.</strong>
        <p>
          The scanner and local review workflow work. Independent target-user
          validation is still pending.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </header>

    <dl class="case-facts shell">
      {projectFacts.map(([term, detail]) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{term}</dt>
          <dd>{detail}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <section class="case-section shell" aria-labelledby="evidence-problem-title">
      <div class="case-section__label">
        <span>00</span>
        <p>The problem</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-section__content">
        <h2 id="evidence-problem-title">The decision is one moment. Its evidence is everywhere.</h2>
        <p>
          Build definitions, tests, ownership, deployment notes, recovery guidance,
          and security reporting often live in different files and different
          people's heads. The accountable person still has to work out what was
          observed, what was not found, what remains unknown, and who owns the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-loop" aria-labelledby="evidence-loop-title">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="case-loop__heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">The working loop</p>
          <h2 id="evidence-loop-title">Scan. Review. Preserve.</h2>
        </div>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <span>01 / Scan</span>
            <h3>Observe a bounded repository.</h3>
            <p>
              Deterministic checks inspect supported local files, cite what they
              find, and explain the search behind evidence they do not observe.
              The default path makes no network request.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>02 / Review</span>
            <h3>Keep judgment visibly human.</h3>
            <p>
              A loopback-only interface separates observed, not observed,
              unknown, and not applicable states. The reviewer can add context,
              ownership, rationale, and follow-up actions.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>03 / Preserve</span>
            <h3>Leave a traceable packet.</h3>
            <p>
              Review state is saved separately from the scanned repository, with
              machine observations and manual assessment kept distinct for the
              person making the decision.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section shell" aria-labelledby="evidence-boundaries-title">
      <div class="case-section__label">
        <span>04</span>
        <p>Boundaries by design</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-section__content">
        <h2 id="evidence-boundaries-title">Useful evidence without a fictional score.</h2>
        <div class="case-decision-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Local by default</h3>
            <p>Repository contents stay on the operator's machine; telemetry and hosted accounts are not required.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Observation, not assurance</h3>
            <p>Finding a file does not prove a practice is effective, and missing evidence is not proof that it does not exist.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>No automated approval</h3>
            <p>The tool does not produce a readiness score, certify compliance, or decide that a release is safe.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Hostile input assumed</h3>
            <p>The scanner does not execute repository content, follows bounded readers, and refuses escaping links.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-outcome" aria-labelledby="evidence-outcome-title">
      <div class="shell case-outcome__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">What exists today</p>
          <h2 id="evidence-outcome-title">Public code. Working workflow. Open question.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            The scanner and local review slice are public under Apache-2.0 and run
            from source. An internal founder baseline exposed workflow problems;
            focused corrections now pass the automated suite.
          </p>
          <p class="case-outcome__limit">
            What this does not prove: independent demand, external usability, a
            safe release decision, or production support. There is no stable
            binary release or commercial offering yet.
          </p>
          <div class="case-outcome__actions">
            <a class="button button--primary" href={repositoryURL} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              View source on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </a>
            <a class="text-link" href="/contact">Discuss the project <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
